*******************************************************************************************                                                                                        **		Apple IIgs Printer Driver                                                        **                                                                                        **		File: spool.a -- psuedo-VM for serial storage/retrieval of PICT data             **                                                                                        **		Written by Ben Koning                                                            **		Copyright (C) 1991 Apple Computer, Inc.                                          **                                                                                        *******************************************************************************************							include		'equates.h'							include		'm16.util'							include		'm16.memory'							include		'e16.GSOS'							include		'm16.GSOS'							include		'm16.misctool'							include		'm16.intmath'							include		'm16.locator'SpoolProc					proc		export**DATA SPOOLING ROUTINES: These routines spool data away to disk, memory, or both.  The*six entry points defined below should remain the same no matter where the data is spooled to.*These routines maintain their own globals in static memory, because their values must be*preserved across print manager calls into this driver.  The author has attempted to keep*these calls as generic (ie, un-printer-driver-specific) as possible.**Entry points, in general order of usage:**	SpoolStartPut			Must be called first; initializes and allocates resources*	SpoolPut				Stores arbitrary number of bytes into psuedo-virtual memory*	SpoolFinishPut			Must be called to declare finish of storage*	SpoolStartGet			Must be called to declare beginning of retrieval*	SpoolGet				Retrieves arbitrary number of bytes from psuedo-virtual memory*	SpoolFinishGet			Must be called last; deallocates all resources**The entry points SPOOLSTARTPUT/SPOOLPUT/SPOOLFINISHPUT and SPOOLSTARTGET/SPOOLGET/SPOOLFINISHGET*are self-explanatory.  On entry "ParamLongB" should have the number of bytes to save/restore,*and "ParamLongC" should have a pointer of where the data will be read/written to.  The accumulator*will be nonzero if an error occurred.  SPOOLGET returns an $FFFF error code if end of data; in*other cases the error code will be that which was returned from a tool call or GS/OS call.**The two routines SPOOLMARKGET/SPOOLRETURNTOMARKGET allow the caller to arrange for the SPOOLGET*routine to repeatedly return to the same point.  A typical scenario would involve a few SPOOLGET*calls, then a SPOOLMARKGET, some more SPOOLGET calls, and then a SPOOLRETURNTOMARKGET which would*cause the next SPOOLGET to retrieve data from the afore-marked position in the spool data.**The spooling strategy is as follows:  Data will be stored in memory until memory starts to*run out, at which time data will be stored to disk, using the memory already allocated as a buffer.*If more memory should become available, it will be used.  While storing data to memory, the Memory*Manager is called at pre-defined intervals (8K, for example) as more memory is needed, using*SetHandleSize.  Before SetHandleSize is called, we see if memory is getting full;*if memory size is a value less than an pre-defined threshold (32K, for example), then*memory is considered to be running out.  This strategy will hopefully avoid memory compaction*and keep tools and other drivers alive.  When the decision to go to disk has been made, a flag*indicating this fact will be set, the entire range of memory used for spooling so far will be*written out, and the spool process will continue, using the amount of memory obtained as a*buffer, which, when full, will be written out to disk.  The disk pathname is "*/SYSTEM/DRIVERS/*TMP.XXXX", where the trailing X's are replaced by a random number.  If such a pathname already*exists, then we try to generate a new name.  If the caller is using their own pathname, then*no such suffix-appending-and-retrying will be done.  If the amount of data to be spooled exceeds*even the size of the big buffer we now have in memory, then it will be written to disk directly.*If the boot volume's FST is AppleShare, then the temporary file is written to the directory*pointed to by the GetUserPath call.**If the caller wants to use its own spool filename, it must store a pointer to its filename in*"SpoolFileNamePtr".  This is done by PrOpenDoc in order to allow the user to specify their own*spool file.  This must be done AFTER calling "SpoolStartPut", which installs a default*filename, which "SpoolPut" later makes sure is unique by trying random suffixes.  Once a filename*has been chosen (by virtue of "SpoolPut" having been called), the rest of the routines will deal*only with that filename, and it should not be modified.  This means that PrPicFile cannot be*called standalone, with an arbitrary filename stuck in the print record, at some arbitrary*time, without first having gone through the PrOpenDoc/PrCloseDoc printing loop.  This limitation*is due to the fact that this is a dynamic, hybrid memory and disk-based spooler.  This limitation*could easily be lifted if the entire document were always spooled entirely to disk.**In a similar fashion, if the caller wants to set their own handle-increment and memory-limit-*threshold values, it must modify the globals "SpoolMemInc" and/or "SpoolMemLeftLimit" AFTER*doing the "SpoolStartPut" call, which installs default values into these static variables.***SPOOLSTARTPUT: Prepare to store data. Call before using SPOOLPUT; ACC->Err:*							export		SpoolStartPut							SpoolStartPut											*Assume standard spool name in case we have to spool to disk:							lda			#SpoolFileName				;assume our standard file name							sta			SpoolFileNamePtr+0			;the user can modify this							lda			#^SpoolFileName				;after this call is completed,							sta			SpoolFileNamePtr+2			;since it doesn't yet open it							*Assume standard handle-grow size policy:							lda			#SpoolMemIncDefault//65536							sta			SpoolMemInc+0							lda			#SpoolMemIncDefault/65536							sta			SpoolMemInc+2							*Assume standard memory-left-go-to-disk threshold policy:							lda			#SpoolMemLeftLimitDefault//65536							sta			SpoolMemLeftLimit+0							lda			#SpoolMemLeftLimitDefault/65536							sta			SpoolMemLeftLimit+2							*Assume we don't have to go to disk:							stz			SpoolToDisk							*Zero our length and pointer variables:							stz			SpoolDataLen+0							stz			SpoolDataLen+2							stz			SpoolHandlePos+0							stz			SpoolHandlePos+2							*Try to get a handle; the first block:							pha										;result space for GetNewID							pushword	#UserIDType					;who is requesting memory							_GetNewID							plx										;save ID in x reg							stx			SpoolPID					;save ID for later disposal							bcs			StartPutRts					;if error, exit with acc set							pha										;result space for NewHandle							pha										;...result space							pushlong	SpoolMemInc					;allocate desired size							phx										;user ID just obtained							pushword	#0							;can be anywhere, move anywhere							pha										;location ptr...							pha										;...will be ignored							_NewHandle							*Store handle for repeated use:							plx							ply							stx			SpoolHandle+0				;save handle in static variable							sty			SpoolHandle+2				;...while preserving acc							bcs			StartPutRts					;if error, exit with acc set*Initialize space left in handle:							lda			SpoolMemInc							sta			SpoolHandleFree							lda			SpoolMemInc+2							sta			SpoolHandleFree+2*Get out:							lda			#0							;no error happenedStartPutRts					rts**SPOOLFINISHPUT: Done storing data. Call after done using SPOOLPUT; ACC->Err:*							export		SpoolFinishPut*If we never went to disk, don't do anything:SpoolFinishPut				lda			SpoolToDisk							beq			FinishPutRts				;exit with acc=0*Flush last chunk out to disk:							jsr			PutDoFlushSubr				;inside "SpoolPut"*Deallocate our user ID and handle:							pushlong	SpoolHandle							_DisposeHandle							pha										;save error status							pushword	SpoolPID					;also remove user ID							_DeleteID*Close our file:							lda			SpoolFileDescriptor			;put ref_num in param block							sta			FinishPutCloseParamsRefNum														_CloseGS	FinishPutCloseParams		;do the OS call														bcs			FinishPutDiskError			;branch if disk error							pla										;else remember MM error statusFinishPutRts				rts										;acc will have error statusFinishPutDiskError			plx										;who cares-disk err important							rts							*Close-call parameter block for GS/OS:FinishPutCloseParams		dc.w		1							;pCountFinishPutCloseParamsRefNum	dc.w		0							;refNum: input**SPOOLPUT: Spools data to disk or memory. Where->ParamLongC; NumBytes->ParamLongB; ACC->Err:*							export		SpoolPut							*Skip most of this code if request is for zero bytes:SpoolPut					lda			ParamLongB+0							ora			ParamLongB+2							bne			PutTryStore							rts												;exit with 0 in ACC*Compare amount of data-requested-to-be-stored with current space left in handle:PutTryStore					lda			ParamLongB+2						;hi order word of request							cmp			SpoolHandleFree+2					;hi order word of free							bcc			PutStoreMemSuccess					;less: it will fit!							beq			PutTryStore1						;equal: test lo words...							bcs			PutTryGrowHandle					;greater: won't fit!PutTryStore1				lda			ParamLongB							;lo order word of request							cmp			SpoolHandleFree						;lo order word of free							beq			PutStoreMemSuccess					;equal: it will fit!							bcc			PutStoreMemSuccess					;less: it will fit!							bra			PutTryGrowHandle					;(greater): won't fit!*Data will fit in current handle!  Deref, add handlepos, store, inc handlepos, dec handlefree:PutStoreMemSuccess			lda			SpoolHandle							;dereference...							sta			ParamLongA							lda			SpoolHandle+2							sta			ParamLongA+2							clc							lda			[ParamLongA]						;get pointer lo word							adc			SpoolHandlePos						;add handle position lo							tax							ldy			#2							lda			[ParamLongA],y						;get pointer hi word							adc			SpoolHandlePos+2					;add handle position hi							sta			ParamLongA+2							stx			ParamLongA							;now ptr to data space							pushlong	ParamLongC							;source							pushlong	ParamLongA							;destination							pushlong	ParamLongB							;count							_BlockMove							clc												;inc handlepos...							lda			SpoolHandlePos							adc			ParamLongB							sta			SpoolHandlePos							lda			SpoolHandlePos+2							adc			ParamLongB+2							sta			SpoolHandlePos+2							sec												;dec handlefree...							lda			SpoolHandleFree							sbc			ParamLongB							sta			SpoolHandleFree							lda			SpoolHandleFree+2							sbc			ParamLongB+2							sta			SpoolHandleFree+2*Increment length of data and exit:PutIncDataLenSuccess		clc												;inc datalen...							lda			SpoolDataLen							adc			ParamLongB							sta			SpoolDataLen							lda			SpoolDataLen+2							adc			ParamLongB+2							sta			SpoolDataLen+2*Exit without error:							lda			#0							rts*Data won't fit in current handle, so see if we have enough space left to enlarge it:PutTryGrowHandle			pha												;result space...							pha							_RealFreeMem							pla							sta			ParamLongA+0							pla							sta			ParamLongA+2							cmp			SpoolMemLeftLimit+2							bcc			PutNoMoreMemory						;less: no more room!							beq			PutTryGrowHandle1					;equal: test lo word...							bra			PutDoGrowHandle						;(greater): ok to grow!PutTryGrowHandle1			lda			ParamLongA							;compare lo words...							cmp			SpoolMemLeftLimit+0							bcc			PutNoMoreMemory						;less: no more room!							beq			PutNoMoreMemory						;equal: no more room!*We have enough memory left in the system, so grow our handle by a chunk:PutDoGrowHandle				pha												;result space...							pha							pushlong	SpoolHandle							_GetHandleSize							clc												;add memory increment...							lda			SpoolMemInc+0						;... right on stack							adc			1,s							sta			1,s							lda			SpoolMemInc+2							adc			3,s							sta			3,s							pushlong	SpoolHandle							_SetHandleSize									;allocate more memory							bcs			PutNoMoreMemory						;ensure call didn't fail							clc												;also inc handlefree...							lda			SpoolHandleFree+0							adc			SpoolMemInc+0							sta			SpoolHandleFree+0							lda			SpoolHandleFree+2							adc			SpoolMemInc+2							sta			SpoolHandleFree+2*Now see if we can store our data in our newly grown memory area:							brl			PutTryStore*Case where can't grow handle bigger, so see if disk flag is set (ie, we have been writing to disk):PutNoMoreMemory				lda			SpoolToDisk							beq			PutTryOpenFile						;not yet, so go open file							brl			PutDoFlush							;yes, so write to disk*Check if user's filename or using our default; do NOT go here after network name-stuff below:PutTryOpenFile				lda			#1							sta			ParamWordA							;assume user filename							lda			SpoolFileNamePtr+0					;theirs or ours?							cmp			#SpoolFileName							beq			PutTryOpenItsOurFileName1							brl			PutTryCreateFile					;its theirsPutTryOpenItsOurFileName1	lda			SpoolFileNamePtr+2							cmp			#^SpoolFileName							beq			PutTryOpenItsOurFileName2							brl			PutTryCreateFile					;its theirsPutTryOpenItsOurFileName2	stz			ParamWordA							;it must be ours!							*If it's our default name, we guarantee uniqueness by repeatedly trying random suffixes:PutTrySuffix				pha												;result space...							pha							_GetTick										;get random number							pla							plx												;use lo word only							phx												;result space...							phx							pha												;pass rnd							_HexIt							pulllong	SpoolFileNameUnique					;store ascii suffix*Also if it's our default name, check if on network; if so modify the path to use USER's directory:PutGenNetName							_DInfoGS	PutDInfoParams						;get boot-dev name							bcs			PutGenNetNameAssNotNetwork														_VolumeGS	PutVolumeParams						;get boot FST ID														bcs			PutGenNetNameAssNotNetwork							lda			PutVolumeParamsFstID							cmp			#$000D								;is it appleshare FST?							beq			PutGenNetNameGoOn					;yes, so continuePutGenNetNameAssNotNetwork	brl			PutDoneModForNetwork				;no, so continuePutGenNetNameGoOn										_FSTSpecific PutGetUsrPathParams				;do FSTSpecific call														bcs			PutGenNetNameAssNotNetwork														lda			PutGetUsrPathParamsPfxPtr+0			;access pathname							sta			ParamLongA+0							lda			PutGetUsrPathParamsPfxPtr+2							sta			ParamLongA+2														lda			[ParamLongA]						;copy pathname...							sta			SpoolNetFileName+0					;copy length word first							shortm							ldy			#2									;y starts beyond len wrds							ldx			#0									;x is count of lenPutGenNetNameCopy			lda			[ParamLongA],y						;copy a byte							sta			SpoolNetFileName,y							iny												;bump address							inx												;bump string count							cpx			SpoolNetFileName+0					;go until length							bne			PutGenNetNameCopy							longm											;y points to next index														shortm											;add ':' separator							lda			#':'							sta			SpoolNetFileName,y							longm							iny														lda			#'T'+('M'*256)						;add 'TMP.'							sta			SpoolNetFileName,y							iny							iny							lda			#'P'+('.'*256)							sta			SpoolNetFileName,y							iny							iny												;y points to next index							sty			ParamLongA+0						;save y for below														pha												;result space							pha												;result space							_GetTick										;get random number							pla							plx												;use lo word only							phx												;result space							phx												;result space							pha												;pass rnd, cvt to ascii,							_HexIt											;leaves 2 words on stack														ldy			ParamLongA+0						;restore y from above							pla												;store 'XXXX' rnd digits							sta			SpoolNetFileName,y							iny							iny							pla							sta			SpoolNetFileName,y					;not maintain y anymore														lda			SpoolNetFileName+0					;we added ':TMP.XXXX',							clc												;9 characters, so adjust							adc			#9									;the string length							sta			SpoolNetFileName+0																					lda			#SpoolNetFileName					;now make sure everyone							sta			SpoolFileNamePtr+0					;uses the built-up name							lda			#^SpoolNetFileName							sta			SpoolFileNamePtr+2PutDoneModForNetwork*Now try to create a file for writing for the first time:PutTryCreateFile			lda			SpoolFileNamePtr+0					;loc of name -> block...							sta			PutCreateParamsFileNamePtr+0							lda			SpoolFileNamePtr+2							sta			PutCreateParamsFileNamePtr+2														_CreateGS	PutCreateParams														bcc			PutCreateSuccess					;go on if successful							ldx			ParamWordA							;was it their filename?							bne			PutExit1							;yes(+err): barf & die							cmp			#dupPathName						;(no): not unique name?							bne			PutExit1							;if some other err, exit							brl			PutTrySuffix						;else, try another namePutExit1					rts												;err in acc, exit*File has been created on disk; now open it for writing and set To-Disk flag:PutCreateSuccess			lda			SpoolFileNamePtr+0					;loc of name -> block...							sta			PutOpenParamsFileNamePtr+0							lda			SpoolFileNamePtr+2							sta			PutOpenParamsFileNamePtr+2														_OpenGS		PutOpenParams														bcs			PutExit1							;err shouldn't happen							lda			PutOpenParamsRefNum					;save ref_num...							sta			SpoolFileDescriptor							lda			#1									;we're now disk-based!							sta			SpoolToDisk*Flush out to disk our filled-up handle memory:PutDoFlush					jsr			PutDoFlushSubr							bcs			PutExit1							;err if disk full?!!							bra			PutContAfterFlush					;else, continue*The following is a subroutine so it can also be used by SpoolFinishPut:PutDoFlushSubr				lda			SpoolFileDescriptor					;ref_num -> block...							sta			PutWriteParamsRefNum							lda			SpoolHandle+0						;deref handle -> block...							sta			ParamLongA+0							lda			SpoolHandle+2							sta			ParamLongA+2							lda			[ParamLongA]						;get ptr lo word...							sta			PutWriteParamsDataPtr+0							ldy			#2									;get ptr hi word...							lda			[ParamLongA],y							sta			PutWriteParamsDataPtr+2							ldy			#4									;lock for OS call...							lda			[ParamLongA],y							ora			#$8000							sta			[ParamLongA],y							lda			SpoolHandlePos+0					;pos (length) -> block...							sta			PutWriteParamsDataLen+0							lda			SpoolHandlePos+2							sta			PutWriteParamsDataLen+2														_WriteGS	PutWriteParams														pha												;save acc error status							ldy			#4									;unlock handle...							lda			[ParamLongA],y							and			#$7FFF							sta			[ParamLongA],y							pla												;restore err status							cmp			#1									;restore carry too							rts*Set our pointers to reflect that we can re-use our handle memory now:PutContAfterFlush			stz			SpoolHandlePos						;pos = 0...							stz			SpoolHandlePos+2							pha												;free = GetHandleSize...							pha							pushlong	SpoolHandle							_GetHandleSize							pulllong	SpoolHandleFree*If we have enough memory to handle store-request into our now-reusable handle-memory, do it:							lda			ParamLongB+2						;how much we need hi word							cmp			SpoolHandleFree+2					;cmp with what we have							beq			PutTryStoreII1						;equal: test lo words							bcs			PutDirectToDisk						;greater: still not enufPutTryStoreIIOk				brl			PutStoreMemSuccess					;less: go store it!PutTryStoreII1				lda			ParamLongB							;how much we need lo word							cmp			SpoolHandleFree						;cmp with what we have							bcc			PutTryStoreIIOk						;less: go store it!*We flushed our buffer to disk but still can't handle data store request; so save directly to disk:PutDirectToDisk				lda			SpoolFileDescriptor					;ref_num -> block...							sta			PutWriteParamsRefNum							lda			ParamLongC+0						;user's ptr -> block...							sta			PutWriteParamsDataPtr+0							lda			ParamLongC+2							sta			PutWriteParamsDataPtr+2							lda			ParamLongB+0						;user's len -> block...							sta			PutWriteParamsDataLen+0							lda			ParamLongB+2							sta			PutWriteParamsDataLen+2														_WriteGS	PutWriteParams														bcs			PutExit2							;err, exit with acc set										brl			PutIncDataLenSuccess				;inc and exit no errPutExit2					rts*Create-call parameter block for GS/OS:PutCreateParams				dc.w		2									;pCountPutCreateParamsFileNamePtr	dc.l		0									;pathnm ptr: input							dc.w		%10000011							;access: --------Drb--iWR							*Open-call parameter block for GS/OS:PutOpenParams				dc.w		2									;pCountPutOpenParamsRefnum			dc.w		0									;ref_num: resultPutOpenParamsFileNamePtr	dc.l		0									;pathnm ptr: input*Write-call parameter block for GS/OS:PutWriteParams				dc.w		5									;pCountPutWriteParamsRefNum		dc.w		0									;ref_num: inputPutWriteParamsDataPtr		dc.l		0									;data_buffer: inputPutWriteParamsDataLen		dc.l		0									;request_count: input							dc.l		0									;transfer_count: result							dc.w		0									;cache_priority: input							*DInfo-call parameter block for GS/OS:PutDInfoParams				dc.w		2									;pCount							dc.w		1									;dev_num: input: 1=boot							dc.l		PutDevName							;dev_name outbuff: input							*Device name storage buffer:PutDevName					dc.w		36									;buffer lengthPutDevName2					dc.w		0									;string length							ds.b		32									;buffer for device name*Volume-call parameter block for GS/OS:PutVolumeParams				dc.w		5									;pCount							dc.l		PutDevName2							;dev_name: input							dc.l		PutVolName							;vol_name outbuff: input							dc.l		0									;total blocks: result							dc.l		0									;free blocks: resultPutVolumeParamsFstID		dc.w		0									;fst_id: result*Volume name storage buffer:PutVolName					dc.w		36									;buffer lengthPutVolName2					dc.w		0									;string length							ds.b		32									;buffer for volume name*FST-specific GetUserPath parameter block for GS/OS:PutGetUsrPathParams			dc.w		3									;pCount							dc.w		$000D								;fst_id: inp: appleshare							dc.w		8									;cmd: input: getuserpathPutGetUsrPathParamsPfxPtr	dc.l		0									;prefix_ptr: result							**SPOOLSTARTGET: Prepare to retrieve data. Call this before using SPOOLGET; ACC->Err:*							export		SpoolStartGet*SpoolHandlePos = 0; Will be used for offset into handle if spooled to memory:SpoolStartGet				stz			SpoolHandlePos+0			;this will be used for offset...							stz			SpoolHandlePos+2			;into handle, if not to-disk*SpoolHandleFree = SpoolDataLen; Will be used to check space left in playback:							lda			SpoolDataLen+0				;this wil be used for making...							sta			SpoolHandleFree+0			;sure caller doesn't request...							lda			SpoolDataLen+2				;more data than stored. note...							sta			SpoolHandleFree+2			;"misuse" of SpoolHandleFree*If we never went to disk, done:							lda			SpoolToDisk					;did we ever go to disk?							beq			StartGetInitMark			;no, and leave 0 in acc for exit*We spooled, so open the current file for reading:							lda			SpoolFileNamePtr+0			;put pointer to pathname							sta			StartGetOpenParamsNamePtr+0	;...into parameter block.  This							lda			SpoolFileNamePtr+2			;pathname might be ours or							sta			StartGetOpenParamsNamePtr+2	;the user's														_OpenGS		StartGetOpenParams														bcs			StartGetExitError			;exit with acc set if error							lda			StartGetOpenParamsRefNum	;get ref_num (file descriptor)							sta			SpoolFileDescriptor							lda			#0							;error didn't happenStartGetInitMark			brl			SpoolMarkGet				;exit to init mark/restore callStartGetExitError			rts*Open-call parameter block for GS/OS:StartGetOpenParams			dc.w		2							;pCountStartGetOpenParamsRefNum	dc.w		0							;ref_num: resultStartGetOpenParamsNamePtr	dc.l		0							;pathname ptr: input**SPOOLFINISHGET: Finished retrieving data. Call when done using SPOOLGET; ACC->Err:*							export		SpoolFinishGet*Decide if we went to disk or memory:SpoolFinishGet				lda			SpoolToDisk							bne			FinishGetWentDisk*Went to memory only, so deallocate handle and user ID:							pushlong	SpoolHandle							_DisposeHandle							pha										;save error status if any							pushword	SpoolPID							_DeleteID							pla										;restore error status							rts										;exit with error status in acc*Went to disk, so close the file:FinishGetWentDisk			lda			SpoolFileDescriptor			;put refnum -> block							sta			FinishGetCloseParamsRefNum														_CloseGS	FinishGetCloseParams		;make the OS call														pha										;save error status*Now delete the file:							lda			SpoolFileNamePtr+0			;put filename ptr -> block							sta			FinishGetDestParamsFNameP+0							lda			SpoolFileNamePtr+2							sta			FinishGetDestParamsFNameP+2														_DestroyGS	FinishGetDestParams			;make the OS call														bcs			FinishGetDestroyErr			;branch if error from DESTROY							pla										;else remember CLOSE err status							rts										;and exitFinishGetDestroyErr			plx										;dump CLOSE err status							rts										;and exit with DESTROY err status							*Close-call parameter block for GS/OS:FinishGetCloseParams		dc.w		1							;pCountFinishGetCloseParamsRefNum	dc.w		0							;refNum: input*Destroy-call parameter block for GS/OS:FinishGetDestParams			dc.w		1							;pCountFinishGetDestParamsFNameP	dc.l		0							;pathname: input**SPOOLGET: Retrieves data. Where->ParamLongC; NumBytesWanted->ParamLongB; ACC->Err ($FFFF=end data):*							export		SpoolGet							*Skip most of this code if the request is for zero bytes:SpoolGet					lda			ParamLongB+0							ora			ParamLongB+2							bne			GetGo1							rts											;exit with 0 in ACC*Make sure there is enough data left to handle the caller's request:GetGo1						lda			ParamLongB+2					;compare hi words							cmp			SpoolHandleFree+2							beq			GetCmpLo						;equal, go compare lo words							bcc			GetCheckSource					;less, go do itGetEndOfData				lda			#$FFFF							;our signal for end of data							rtsGetCmpLo					lda			ParamLongB						;compare lo words							cmp			SpoolHandleFree							beq			GetCheckSource					;equal, ok							bcs			GetEndOfData					;greater, not ok*Decide if the data is on disk or in memory:GetCheckSource				lda			SpoolToDisk							bne			GetFromDisk*Data in memory; deref+add offset and blockmove the data; no need to lock because no OS calls:							lda			SpoolHandle						;dereference...							sta			ParamLongA							lda			SpoolHandle+2							sta			ParamLongA+2							clc							lda			[ParamLongA]					;get pointer lo word							adc			SpoolHandlePos					;add handle position lo							tax							ldy			#2							lda			[ParamLongA],y					;get pointer hi word							adc			SpoolHandlePos+2				;add handle position hi							pha											;hi word source							phx											;lo word source							pushlong	ParamLongC						;destination							pushlong	ParamLongB						;count							_BlockMove							clc											;inc handlepos...							lda			SpoolHandlePos							adc			ParamLongB							sta			SpoolHandlePos							lda			SpoolHandlePos+2							adc			ParamLongB+2							sta			SpoolHandlePos+2							bra			GetDecExitSuccess*Data on disk; just read requested bytes.  GS/OS's caching will help performance here:GetFromDisk					lda			SpoolFileDescriptor				;ref_num...							sta			GetReadParamsRefNum				;-> block							lda			ParamLongC+0					;request buffer...							sta			GetReadParamsBuffPtr+0			;-> block							lda			ParamLongC+2							sta			GetReadParamsBuffPtr+2							lda			ParamLongB+0					;request length...							sta			GetReadParamsCount+0			;-> block							lda			ParamLongB+2							sta			GetReadParamsCount+2														_ReadGS		GetReadParams														bcs			GetRts1							;err, exit with acc set*Successful exit in both cases; dec SpoolHandleFree, which is relevant even if reading from disk:GetDecExitSuccess			sec							lda			SpoolHandleFree					;subtract lo word							sbc			ParamLongB							sta			SpoolHandleFree							lda			SpoolHandleFree+2				;subtract hi word							sbc			ParamLongB+2							sta			SpoolHandleFree+2							lda			#0								;no errorGetRts1						rts*Read-call parameter block for GS/OS:GetReadParams				dc.w		5								;pCountGetReadParamsRefNum			dc.w		0								;ref_num: inputGetReadParamsBuffPtr		dc.l		0								;data_buffer: inputGetReadParamsCount			dc.l		0								;request_count: input							dc.l		0								;transfer_count: result							dc.w		1								;cache_priority**SPOOLMARKGET: Sets mark in spool data to which we can come back later.  Use only during playback:*							export		SpoolMarkGet*Save current position:SpoolMarkGet				lda			SpoolHandlePos+0							sta			SpoolMarkHandlePos+0							lda			SpoolHandlePos+2							sta			SpoolMarkHandlePos+2*Save current space left:							lda			SpoolHandleFree+0							sta			SpoolMarkHandleFree+0							lda			SpoolHandleFree+2							sta			SpoolMarkHandleFree+2*If we didn't go to disk, we're done:							lda			SpoolToDisk							beq			MarkGetDone*Save current file pointer location:							lda			SpoolFileDescriptor							sta			MarkGetParamsRefNum														_GetMarkGS	MarkGetParams														lda			MarkGetParamsPosition+0							sta			SpoolMarkDiskPos+0							lda			MarkGetParamsPosition+2							sta			SpoolMarkDiskPos+2*Exit:							MarkGetDone					rts*GetMark-call parameter block for GS/OS:MarkGetParams				dc.w		2								;pCountMarkGetParamsRefNum			dc.w		0								;ref_num: inputMarkGetParamsPosition		dc.l		0								;position: result**SPOOLRETURNTOMARKGET: Restores position to mark set above. Only for playback:*							export		SpoolReturnToMarkGet*Restore previous position:SpoolReturnToMarkGet		lda			SpoolMarkHandlePos+0							sta			SpoolHandlePos+0							lda			SpoolMarkHandlePos+2							sta			SpoolHandlePos+2*Restore previous space left:							lda			SpoolMarkHandleFree+0							sta			SpoolHandleFree+0							lda			SpoolMarkHandleFree+2							sta			SpoolHandleFree+2*If we didn't go to disk, we're done:							lda			SpoolToDisk							beq			MarkReturnDone*Restore previous file pointer location:							lda			SpoolFileDescriptor							sta			MarkReturnParamsRefNum							lda			SpoolMarkDiskPos+0							sta			MarkReturnParamsPosition+0							lda			SpoolMarkDiskPos+2							sta			MarkReturnParamsPosition+2														_SetMarkGS	MarkReturnParams							*Exit:							MarkReturnDone				rts*SetMark-call parameter block for GS/OS:MarkReturnParams			dc.w		3								;pCountMarkReturnParamsRefNum		dc.w		0								;ref_num: input							dc.w		0								;base: inputMarkReturnParamsPosition	dc.l		0								;position: input**DATA: Static globals which needs to be preserved across calls to these routines:*Note: SpoolDataSize is kept valid even if spooling is going to disk!*							export		SpoolFileNamePtr					;user modifyable							export		SpoolMemInc							;user modifyable							export		SpoolMemLeftLimit					;user modifyableSpoolDataLen				dc.l		0									;total length of dataSpoolHandle					dc.l		0									;handle to dataSpoolHandleFree				dc.l		0									;space left in handleSpoolHandlePos				dc.l		0									;current data positionSpoolMarkHandlePos			dc.l		0									;for mark/returnSpoolMarkHandleFree			dc.l		0									;for mark/returnSpoolMarkDiskPos			dc.l		0									;for mark/returnSpoolMemInc					dc.l		0									;amount to grow handleSpoolMemLeftLimit			dc.l		0									;go-to-disk thresholdSpoolToDisk					dc.w		0									;1=if spooling to diskSpoolFileDescriptor			dc.w		0									;"ref_num" for GS/OSSpoolPID					dc.w		0									;user ID for malloc callsSpoolFileNamePtr			dc.l		0									;ptr to actual spool nameSpoolFileName				dc.w		SpoolFileNameEnd-SpoolFileNameStart	;our default spool nameSpoolFileNameStart			dc.b		'*/SYSTEM/DRIVERS/TMP.'				;this part stays sameSpoolFileNameUnique			dc.b		'XXXX'								;this gets rand suffixSpoolFileNameEndSpoolNetFileName			dc.w		0									;gets built-up name							ds.b		80							endproc							end